Without -r, `md list` must look at the top level of the directory only. The
fixture holds notes/one.md and notes/deeper/two.md, so the answer is one file.

This is the pair of list-recurses-into-a-subdirectory. Together they prove
that -r is what makes the difference, which neither case can show alone: the
same tree gives one file without the flag and two with it.

The output cannot be pinned byte for byte, because `list` prints an absolute
path that holds the scratch directory name. See the sibling case for why the
block count stands in for the file count.
